{
 "id": "2019-wang-park",
 "name": "Park Walkway Features: Paired Photo Choices",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the paper's own stimuli: participants see 28 pairs of Nanjing park walkway photos. In each pair the two photos are the same except for one digitally added or removed feature: ground cover plants, benches with backs and arms, colourful flowers, available seating, a diverse mix of plants, visual access to water, or canopy trees (four pairs per feature). They imagine walking in the park and tap the walkway they would prefer to use. The 28 pairs are those printed in Appendix Table A1 of Wang & Rodiek (2019), IJERPH 16(20): 3808 (CC BY 4.0), and the paper's per-pair and per-feature percentages are stored for comparison. Scoring: per feature, the share of its four pairs where the photo with the feature was chosen, tested against 50%. Differences from the original study: (1) online instead of printed side-by-side booklets handed out in six parks (on phones the two photos are stacked); (2) the paper recruited local residents aged 60 and over, and the template does not restrict age; (3) pair order and left/right sides are random per participant, instead of two booklet versions with fixed random sides; (4) photos are the small versions printed in the paper (about 310 px wide).",
 "author": "Wang & Rodiek",
 "year": "2019",
 "category": "Academic Research",
